# Frozen gotcha quiz for tools/probe.py. One question per reference claim that a rewrite might cut as "already known".
# id, source (file:line in the pre-v7 reference), question sent to the model, answer_key kept for the human grader.
{"id": "hooks-01", "source": "hooks.md:66", "question": "In a Claude Code hook script, which exit codes block the action, and what happens to the action when the hook exits 1?", "answer_key": "Only exit 2 blocks. Exit 1 (or any other nonzero) is a non-blocking error: the action proceeds and a hook-error notice appears in the transcript. Exception: WorktreeCreate, where any nonzero exit fails creation."}
{"id": "hooks-02", "source": "hooks.md:68", "question": "A Claude Code PreToolUse hook prints a JSON object with permissionDecision: \"deny\" to stdout and then exits 2. What does Claude Code do with the JSON?", "answer_key": "The JSON is discarded. stdout is parsed as JSON only on exit 0; on exit 2 only stderr is read as the block reason. The two channels are mutually exclusive."}
{"id": "hooks-03", "source": "hooks.md:70", "question": "In a Claude Code settings.json hook, the matcher is written as \"Edit.*\". Which tools does it match, and why?", "answer_key": "Any character outside letters, digits, _, -, whitespace, comma or pipe turns the matcher into an unanchored regex tested with RegExp.test, so \"Edit.*\" also matches NotebookEdit. Anchor as ^Edit$ to match only Edit."}
{"id": "hooks-04", "source": "hooks.md:72", "question": "In a Claude Code hook, the matcher is \"mcp__myserver\" with no trailing pattern. Which MCP tools from that server does it match?", "answer_key": "None. It contains only exact-match characters, so it is compared as a literal tool name, and no tool is literally named mcp__myserver. Use mcp__myserver__.* to match the server's tools. This is a silent zero-match."}
{"id": "hooks-05", "source": "hooks.md:74", "question": "In Claude Code, a user types @secrets/config.yaml in their prompt. Does a PreToolUse hook matched on the Read tool fire? How should sensitive file contents be protected instead?", "answer_key": "No. @file references are expanded while building the prompt with no Read tool call, so no PreToolUse hook fires. Use a Read permission deny rule, which applies to @-references directly."}
{"id": "hooks-06", "source": "hooks.md:76", "question": "In Claude Code, a PostToolUse hook is matched on Edit|Write to catch every file modification. Claude runs `sed -i` via Bash. Does the hook see it, and what are two ways to compensate?", "answer_key": "No, a Bash-driven edit never trips an Edit|Write matcher. Compensate by including Bash in the matcher and inspecting tool_input.command, or by a Stop-time hook running git status --porcelain once per turn."}
{"id": "hooks-07", "source": "hooks.md:78", "question": "In Claude Code, can a PreToolUse hook that returns permissionDecision \"allow\" override a deny rule in settings? And does a hook's deny still hold in bypassPermissions mode?", "answer_key": "A hook allow never overrides a deny or ask rule from any settings scope. A hook deny (or exit 2) holds even in bypassPermissions mode, because it runs before permission rules are evaluated."}
{"id": "hooks-08", "source": "hooks.md:80", "question": "In Claude Code, two PreToolUse hooks match the same tool call and both return updatedInput. Which one's input is used?", "answer_key": "They run in parallel and the last one to finish wins, non-deterministically. Never generate two input-rewriting hooks for one tool; combine them into one script."}
{"id": "hooks-09", "source": "hooks.md:82", "question": "In Claude Code, what input field must a Stop hook check to avoid an infinite loop, and what built-in cap exists on consecutive Stop-hook blocks?", "answer_key": "It must read stop_hook_active and let the session stop when it is true. Claude Code caps consecutive Stop-hook blocks at 8 (CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP overrides; 0 disables)."}
{"id": "hooks-10", "source": "hooks.md:84", "question": "In Claude Code, a hook injects additionalContext. Should the text be phrased as an instruction (\"Run bun test\") or as a fact (\"This repo uses bun test\"), and why does it matter?", "answer_key": "As a fact. additionalContext is injected as an invisible system reminder, and imperative phrasing can trip Claude's prompt-injection defenses so the text is surfaced to the user instead of acted on."}
{"id": "hooks-11", "source": "hooks.md:86-92", "question": "In Claude Code, what are the default hook timeouts for UserPromptSubmit, MessageDisplay and SessionEnd, and what is unusual about SessionEnd's?", "answer_key": "UserPromptSubmit 30s, MessageDisplay 10s, SessionEnd 1.5s. SessionEnd's is a budget shared across all SessionEnd hooks, not per hook; a per-hook timeout raises the budget up to 60s, and CLAUDE_CODE_SESSIONEND_HOOKS_TIMEOUT_MS overrides it. Most other hooks default to 600s."}
{"id": "hooks-12", "source": "hooks.md:94", "question": "A Claude Code command hook in shell form prints valid JSON, but Claude Code fails to parse it. The user's .zshrc echoes a banner on startup. What is happening?", "answer_key": "The shell sources the profile, whose stdout lands before the hook's JSON and breaks parsing. Guard profile output with an interactive-shell check ([[ $- == *i* ]]) or use exec form with args. Hooks also have no tty."}
{"id": "hooks-13", "source": "hooks.md:96", "question": "In Claude Code, what minimum versions gate the hook `if` field, comma-separated matcher lists, and hyphens inside exact-match matchers? What happened to a hyphenated matcher like code-reviewer before that last one?", "answer_key": "if: v2.1.85+; comma lists: v2.1.191+; hyphens in exact matchers: v2.1.195+. Before that, a hyphenated name silently became an unanchored regex that also matched senior-code-reviewer."}
{"id": "hooks-14", "source": "hooks.md:102", "question": "In Claude Code permissions, a deny rule Bash(aws *) and an allow rule Bash(aws s3 ls) both exist. Which wins for `aws s3 ls`, and why?", "answer_key": "Deny wins. Rules are evaluated deny, then ask, then allow, and the first match decides regardless of specificity or scope. An exception must be carved out of the deny pattern itself."}
{"id": "hooks-15", "source": "hooks.md:106", "question": "In Claude Code Read/Edit permission rules, what do the path prefixes //, ~/, a single /, and a bare filename each resolve against?", "answer_key": "// is absolute from the filesystem root; ~/ is the home directory; a single / is relative to the settings file's own project root (so the same text resolves differently in project vs user settings); a bare filename or ./path is relative to cwd, and a bare name like .env matches at any depth."}
{"id": "hooks-16", "source": "hooks.md:108-110", "question": "In Claude Code, does a permission rule Write(docs/**) protect docs/? Which tools does an Edit(path) rule govern, and a Read(path) rule?", "answer_key": "No. File permission checks consult Edit(path) and Read(path) rules only; Write/NotebookEdit/Glob/MultiEdit path rules are accepted and never consulted (a startup warning exists since v2.1.210). Edit governs Write and NotebookEdit; Read governs Grep, Glob and IDE file context."}
{"id": "hooks-17", "source": "hooks.md:114-116", "question": "On a fresh, untrusted clone of a repo, which parts of a Claude Code project harness are inert until the user accepts workspace trust, and which stay active?", "answer_key": "Trust gates what the repo grants: permissions.allow, additionalDirectories, project hooks, project statusLine, a project skill's allowed-tools, project subagent frontmatter hooks, autoMemoryDirectory. Deny and ask rules stay active. The skipped hook is only noted in the debug log."}
{"id": "hooks-18", "source": "hooks.md:122-134", "question": "In Claude Code, can an allow rule like Edit(.claude/**) pre-approve writes under .claude/? What happens to such writes in dontAsk mode?", "answer_key": "No. .claude is a protected path and the safety check runs before allow rules, so the rule does nothing. In dontAsk mode protected-path writes are denied outright; default/acceptEdits prompt; bypassPermissions allows."}
{"id": "hooks-19", "source": "hooks.md:138", "question": "In Claude Code, what happens to a broad allow rule like Bash(*) or Bash(python*) when the session enters auto mode?", "answer_key": "Broad allow rules granting arbitrary code execution are suspended in auto mode (also Agent allow rules and package-manager run commands). Narrow rules like Bash(npm test) carry over."}
{"id": "hooks-20", "source": "hooks.md:142", "question": "In Claude Code, a project's .claude/settings.json sets permissions.defaultMode to \"auto\". What mode does the session start in?", "answer_key": "default. Since v2.1.142 a project or local settings file's defaultMode: auto is ignored with no error or warning; only ~/.claude/settings.json or managed settings can set auto."}
{"id": "hooks-21", "source": "hooks.md:148-150", "question": "In Claude Code permissions, does the allow rule Bash(npm test) approve `npm test && rm -rf build` without a prompt? And what is the difference between Bash(ls *) and Bash(ls*)?", "answer_key": "No: each sub-command of a compound command must independently match an allow rule. Bash(ls *) requires a space or end after ls (a word boundary), so it does not match lsof; Bash(ls*) matches lsof too."}
{"id": "hooks-22", "source": "hooks.md:52", "question": "In Claude Code, where is the hook handler field once: true honored, and where is it silently ignored?", "answer_key": "Honored only for hooks declared in a skill's frontmatter (runs once per session then removes itself). Ignored on agent-frontmatter hooks and settings.json hooks."}
{"id": "hooks-23", "source": "hooks.md:54", "question": "In Claude Code, a hook declared in a skill's frontmatter has command: ./scripts/check.sh. What directory is that path relative to, and which ${...} substitutions are documented for hook command fields?", "answer_key": "Relative to the skill's own directory, not the project root. Documented substitutions are ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}, ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}, ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}; ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR} is not documented as substituted in a hook command."}
{"id": "hooks-24", "source": "hooks.md:229", "question": "In Claude Code, a Stop hook is given \"async\": true to keep it from slowing turns. Can it still block Claude from stopping? What does asyncRewake do?", "answer_key": "No. An async hook's output is ignored entirely (no stdout, JSON or exit code), so it cannot block. asyncRewake: true runs in the background and wakes Claude on exit 2, a middle path for slow checks."}
{"id": "agents-01", "source": "agents.md:53", "question": "In Claude Code, when a custom subagent defined in .claude/agents/*.md runs, what happens to Claude Code's default system prompt?", "answer_key": "It is replaced entirely by the agent's markdown body. Only the body, bare environment details, and whatever CLAUDE.md/skills load through normal mechanisms carry over; default behaviours like 'run tests before done' do not."}
{"id": "agents-02", "source": "agents.md:59", "question": "In Claude Code, do the built-in Explore and Plan subagents load the project's CLAUDE.md and git status? Can this be changed per agent?", "answer_key": "No, both skip the CLAUDE.md hierarchy and git status by design, and there is no frontmatter field to change it. Every other built-in and custom subagent does load them."}
{"id": "agents-03", "source": "agents.md:71", "question": "In Claude Code, an agent's frontmatter declares a Stop hook. Which event actually fires it at runtime?", "answer_key": "It is automatically treated as SubagentStop: it fires when that agent stops, not when the main session stops."}
{"id": "agents-04", "source": "agents.md:73", "question": "In Claude Code (v2.1.218+), what happens to hooks declared in a project agent's frontmatter on an untrusted fresh clone? Do agents in ~/.claude/agents/ behave the same?", "answer_key": "The agent still runs but its frontmatter hooks are skipped until the folder is trusted, with only a debug-log trace. User-level agents in ~/.claude/agents/ are exempt."}
{"id": "agents-05", "source": "agents.md:77", "question": "In Claude Code, two files in .claude/agents/ declare the same frontmatter name. What happens, and which field is an agent's identity: name or filename?", "answer_key": "Identity is the frontmatter name, never the filename. On a collision only one loads, silently, with no error or warning."}
{"id": "agents-06", "source": "agents.md:81", "question": "In Claude Code, a custom subagent lists AskUserQuestion in its tools frontmatter. Can the subagent ask the user a question?", "answer_key": "No. AskUserQuestion is tied to the main conversation's UI and is unavailable in any subagent regardless of the tools list."}
{"id": "agents-07", "source": "agents.md:93", "question": "In Claude Code, a subagent's tools frontmatter lists Read, Grep, Glob, Bash. Can it invoke project skills via the Skill tool?", "answer_key": "No. An explicit tools allowlist does not imply Skill; omitting Skill is the documented way to stop a subagent invoking skills, and nothing announces it at runtime. Name Skill explicitly if needed."}
{"id": "agents-08", "source": "agents.md:95", "question": "In Claude Code, what does listing a skill under a subagent's skills: frontmatter field inject at startup: the skill's description or its full body?", "answer_key": "Its complete body, at every invocation, not the description. List only skills the agent needs in full on essentially every run."}
{"id": "agents-09", "source": "agents.md:99", "question": "In Claude Code, a read-only subagent with tools: Read, Grep, Glob gets memory: project. What does enabling memory do to its tool set, and where do project-scope notes live?", "answer_key": "Enabling memory automatically enables Read, Write and Edit, which can hand back write access the allowlist removed; the docs do not say which wins. project scope creates a version-controlled .claude/agent-memory/<name>/."}
{"id": "agents-10", "source": "agents.md:33", "question": "In Claude Code, when a subagent is loaded from a plugin, which frontmatter fields are ignored?", "answer_key": "hooks, mcpServers and permissionMode are ignored for plugin-loaded agents, for security reasons."}
{"id": "agents-11", "source": "agents.md:24-27", "question": "In Claude Code agent teams: are they on by default, do teammates get worktree isolation, and can per-teammate permission modes be set at spawn?", "answer_key": "Off by default (CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS must be set; otherwise Claude does not spawn or propose teammates). Teammates are not isolated in worktrees. Permissions start from the lead's; per-teammate modes cannot be set at spawn but can be changed afterward."}
{"id": "agents-12", "source": "agents.md:29", "question": "In Claude Code, a subagent definition with skills: and mcpServers: fields is run as an agent-team teammate. Are those fields applied?", "answer_key": "No. skills: and mcpServers: are not applied when the definition runs as a teammate; a teammate loads skills and MCP servers from project and user settings like an ordinary session."}
{"id": "agents-13", "source": "agents.md:96", "question": "In Claude Code, what happens to a subagent's permissionMode frontmatter field when the parent session is in auto mode?", "answer_key": "It is ignored entirely; every subagent action goes through the classifier with the parent's rules. It also can only tighten a default-mode parent, never loosen a locked-down one."}
{"id": "cmr-01", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:7", "question": "In Claude Code, how is CLAUDE.md content delivered to the model: as part of the system prompt or otherwise? Does Claude Code enforce compliance with it?", "answer_key": "As a user message injected after the system prompt, not as the system prompt. There is no enforcement guarantee; contradictions are resolved arbitrarily."}
{"id": "cmr-02", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:68", "question": "In Claude Code, if CLAUDE.md is edited during a live session, when does the change take effect?", "answer_key": "Not until /clear, /compact, or a restart; CLAUDE.md is read once at session start."}
{"id": "cmr-03", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:69", "question": "In Claude Code, the user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md says 'always use tabs' and the project CLAUDE.md says 'use 2-space indentation'. Which wins?", "answer_key": "Neither. All CLAUDE.md files concatenate broad-to-specific with no override mechanism; both are in context and the model may pick either."}
{"id": "cmr-04", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:70", "question": "In Claude Code, does writing @README.md in CLAUDE.md lazy-load the file when needed, or load it at session start? How do you mention a file without importing it, and what is the import depth cap?", "answer_key": "It expands at launch every session, saving no context. Wrap the mention in backticks to avoid importing. Recursive imports are capped at 4 hops."}
{"id": "cmr-05", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:71", "question": "In Claude Code, does an HTML comment <!-- like this --> in CLAUDE.md reach the model's context?", "answer_key": "No. Block-level HTML comments are stripped before injection, so they are free maintainer notes."}
{"id": "cmr-06", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:72", "question": "In Claude Code, a file is placed in .claude/rules/ without a paths: frontmatter key. When does it load?", "answer_key": "At launch, with the same priority as CLAUDE.md itself. Only a paths: glob makes a rule conditional, loading when Claude reads a matching file. Globs support brace expansion."}
{"id": "cmr-07", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:74-82", "question": "In Claude Code, after a compaction, which instruction surfaces are re-injected automatically and which are not: project-root CLAUDE.md, a rule without paths:, a subdirectory CLAUDE.md, a rule with paths:?", "answer_key": "Project-root CLAUDE.md (and CLAUDE.local.md) and rules without paths: are re-read and re-injected. A subdirectory CLAUDE.md and a paths:-scoped rule are not, until Claude next reads a matching file."}
{"id": "cmr-08", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:54", "question": "In Claude Code auto memory, how much of MEMORY.md loads into every conversation, where does it live, and can it be turned off?", "answer_key": "The first 200 lines or 25KB, whichever comes first. It lives in ~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/. It can be disabled with autoMemoryEnabled: false or CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1, and it never travels between machines or into subagents."}
{"id": "cmr-09", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:60", "question": "Does Claude Code read AGENTS.md? A repo already has one; what is the recommended way to make Claude use it without duplicating content?", "answer_key": "No, Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md, not AGENTS.md. Make @AGENTS.md the first line of CLAUDE.md (or symlink) rather than copying content."}
{"id": "cmr-10", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:84", "question": "In Claude Code auto permission mode, does a prohibition written in CLAUDE.md prose affect which actions are allowed?", "answer_key": "Yes, partially: the auto-mode classifier reads CLAUDE.md and prose prohibitions steer its allow/block decisions, though this is not a hard guarantee; pair with a permissions.deny rule."}
{"id": "cmr-11", "source": "claude-md-and-rules.md:52", "question": "In Claude Code, where does CLAUDE.local.md sit, when does it load relative to CLAUDE.md, and what is the caveat for git worktrees?", "answer_key": "At the project root, gitignored, loading right after CLAUDE.md. A gitignored file exists only in the worktree that created it, so a fact that should follow the developer belongs in their home directory via @~/... import."}
{"id": "skills-01", "source": "skills.md:62", "question": "In Claude Code, a skill at .claude/skills/deploy-staging/SKILL.md has frontmatter name: Deploy to Staging. What slash command invokes it?", "answer_key": "/deploy-staging. The directory name is the command; frontmatter name is a display label only."}
{"id": "skills-02", "source": "skills.md:64", "question": "In Claude Code, a skill's frontmatter YAML fails to parse. Does the skill still load, and can it still auto-trigger?", "answer_key": "The body still loads with empty metadata and /skill-name works by direct invocation, but with no description it never auto-triggers again, silently."}
{"id": "skills-03", "source": "skills.md:66", "question": "In Claude Code, after /compact, is the skill listing re-injected? What happens to a skill that never triggered before compaction?", "answer_key": "No, /compact does not re-inject the skill listing; an untriggered skill is gone from the model's awareness for the rest of the session. Back critical behaviours with a CLAUDE.md trigger line."}
{"id": "skills-04", "source": "skills.md:68", "question": "In a Claude Code SKILL.md, what does the !`command` syntax do, and can the model decide not to run it?", "answer_key": "It is preprocessing: the shell command runs before the skill content is sent and its stdout replaces the placeholder. The model receives only the output and cannot conditionally skip it; it always runs when the skill loads."}
{"id": "skills-05", "source": "skills.md:70", "question": "In Claude Code, do edits to a SKILL.md take effect within the current session? Do edits to CLAUDE.md?", "answer_key": "SKILL.md edits take effect immediately in the current session. CLAUDE.md edits do not until /clear, /compact or restart."}
{"id": "skills-06", "source": "skills.md:44", "question": "In Claude Code, roughly what share of the context window is the skill-description listing budget by default, and what happens to skill descriptions when the listing overflows it?", "answer_key": "About 1% of the context window (configurable). On overflow, the least-invoked skills' descriptions collapse to bare names, silently disabling their auto-triggering."}
{"id": "skills-07", "source": "skills.md:50", "question": "In Claude Code, at how many characters is a skill's combined description + when_to_use truncated in the skill listing?", "answer_key": "1,536 characters. Put the triggering-critical clause first."}
{"id": "skills-08", "source": "skills.md:30", "question": "In Claude Code, does a 'When to use this skill' section in a SKILL.md body affect whether the skill auto-triggers?", "answer_key": "No. Only the frontmatter description (plus when_to_use) decides triggering; the body loads only after the decision to trigger."}
{"id": "skills-09", "source": "skills.md:51", "question": "In Claude Code, what does disable-model-invocation: true do to a skill's auto-triggering, its context budget cost, and its /name invocation?", "answer_key": "It removes the skill from auto-triggering and from the context budget entirely; only explicit /name invokes it."}
{"id": "skills-10", "source": "skills.md:54", "question": "In Claude Code, what does context: fork with an agent: field do to a skill's body, and why pair it with allowed-tools?", "answer_key": "The body becomes the subagent's entire prompt, run in isolation rather than inline. allowed-tools keeps the forked run from stalling on a permission prompt mid-flight."}
{"id": "wf-01", "source": "workflows.md:33", "question": "In a Claude Code dynamic workflow (.claude/workflows/*.js), what constraint applies to the exported meta object, and why?", "answer_key": "It must be a pure literal: no variables, calls or template interpolation. The runtime extracts meta by reading the file before executing anything."}
{"id": "wf-02", "source": "workflows.md:34", "question": "In a Claude Code workflow script, what happens if it calls Date.now(), Math.random(), or new Date() with no arguments?", "answer_key": "They are rejected outright (breaks resume determinism). Anything varying per run must arrive via args or be stamped on after the workflow returns."}
{"id": "wf-03", "source": "workflows.md:35", "question": "In Claude Code, a project has .claude/workflows/release.js and the user has ~/.claude/workflows/release.js. Which runs for /release in that project?", "answer_key": "The project-scope workflow shadows the personal one on a name collision."}
{"id": "wf-04", "source": "workflows.md:36", "question": "In Claude Code, what permission mode do agents spawned by a dynamic workflow run in, and what happens when one needs a Bash command not in permissions.allow?", "answer_key": "acceptEdits, unconditionally, regardless of the session's mode. There is no interactive prompt mid-run, so the run stalls on the permission prompt until approved out-of-band; add the allow entries with the workflow."}
{"id": "wf-05", "source": "workflows.md:37", "question": "In Claude Code, what gates whether dynamic workflows are available at all (version, plan, opt-in, disable switches)?", "answer_key": "Claude Code v2.1.154+, a paid plan, on Pro an opt-in via the Dynamic workflows row in /config, and they can be disabled via disableWorkflows in settings or CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_WORKFLOWS."}
{"id": "wf-06", "source": "workflows.md:39-40", "question": "In a Claude Code workflow script, can the script itself read files or run shell commands? What are the concurrency caps?", "answer_key": "No: the script cannot touch the filesystem or shell; only the agent() calls it spawns can. Caps are 16 simultaneous agents and 1,000 total per run."}
{"id": "wf-07", "source": "e2e-testing.md:83", "question": "In Claude Code, is ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR} substituted inside a dynamic workflow's prompt strings or in a subagent's shell environment?", "answer_key": "No. It is substituted in a skill's own markdown body and in allowed-tools rules only; in a workflow prompt string or a subagent's shell it arrives as literal text (or expands to nothing)."}
{"id": "wf-08", "source": "e2e-testing.md:83", "question": "In a Claude Code workflow, an agent() call has no schema option. What does it return, and what happens if you read run.label off it?", "answer_key": "It returns its final text as a plain string, so reading a property yields undefined silently. Attach a schema or carry identity from the original item."}
{"id": "e2e-01", "source": "e2e-testing.md:168", "question": "In Claude Code, is AskUserQuestion available in headless (-p) sessions or inside subagents?", "answer_key": "No, in neither. Interview-style flows cannot be exercised headlessly; only interactive dogfooding can."}
{"id": "e2e-02", "source": "hooks-events.md:315", "question": "In Claude Code, does the PermissionRequest hook event fire in -p (headless) mode? What should be used there instead?", "answer_key": "No, PermissionRequest does not fire in -p mode; use PreToolUse there."}
{"id": "misc-01", "source": "SKILL.md:86", "question": "In Claude Code, what is loaded into context on every request before the first prompt, at the project level? Name the components of the always-loaded bill.", "answer_key": "CLAUDE.md plus every rule without paths:, plus every @import expanded, plus the first 200 lines of auto memory's MEMORY.md; skill descriptions sit in a separate ~1% listing budget."}
{"id": "misc-02", "source": "SKILL.md:112", "question": "In Claude Code, where is ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR} substituted, and where does it expand to nothing?", "answer_key": "Substituted in the skill's own markdown and in allowed-tools rules; not in a workflow's prompt strings or a subagent's shell environment, where it expands to nothing."}
